The ST-V arcade board: its BIOS comes out of a set, its EEPROM is save data,
and its reads of the controls are told.

The ST-V is the Saturn's hardware as an arcade board, and Mednafen runs it as
the Saturn system. Three things of it need a word from the frontend:

- The BIOS. Mednafen reads it from a file of its own. MAME keeps the BIOS of
  every region in one zip (stvbios.zip), and that is the file people have, so
  the frontend is asked for the bytes first (MDFN_ChimeraFirmware) and the
  file is read as before when it gives none. The Saturn's BIOS is not
  changed: the frontend answers for the three ST-V settings only.
- The EEPROM, where the board keeps its settings. It is told to the frontend
  as a save memory ("seep"), as the backup memory already is, so Export Save
  Data writes it and a savestate holds it. Mednafen's own loading of the file
  at the start is kept.
- The controls. A game reads the sticks, the buttons, the coin and the start
  switches through the board's I/O chip and not through the Saturn's pad
  ports, so a frame is not a lag frame when one of those is read.

